  "notes": "The crew record — Mannie Fresh as sole producer, four rappers, and the densest keyboard writing in the Cash Money catalogue. ⛔ **Density is the distinctive, and it is the sourced one:** \"two or three simultaneous keyboard lines (lead motif, counter-line, pad) — **denser than crunk**\". So `melody.densityPerBar` is raised, `emptyBarBias` drops to 0.03 (the lowest anywhere on this axis — the parent's 0.06 was already the lowest against crunk's 0.15), and `countermelody.densityRatio` reaches 0.85 with all three styles live, because the counter-line and the pad are two real parts and not an ornament. ⛔ **Mixed major and minor across one record**, which no other entry in this lane does: the research says \"mixed major/minor\", so `session.scales` and `progressionFamilies` both carry upper- and lower-case families side by side, and `chords.impliedByRiff` is set because the harmony under them is \"single-chord vamps with keyboard countermelodies\" — the keyboard figure states the chord, there is no pad announcing changes. The variety that a static harmony would otherwise cost is bought with a wide `voicing.register`, a 2–12 beat `chordDurationBeats` and three `harmonicRhythm` values rather than with progressions the records do not play. ⚠ **SP-1200 grit** is the humanize block: 12-bit drum layers on some tracks, so `quantizeStrength` drops to 0.83 and `velocityVar` rises to 0.19 — looser than the parent, which is already the loosest thing in this family. ⚠ **No bridge, and long instrumental outros for DJ use** — the arrangement drops every bridge-bearing structure and lets `outro` reach 24 bars, which nothing else in this batch does. Verses run 8–16 because four rappers trade. ⚠ **Confidence `medium` because the research rates it medium-high** and marks the 90–102 band **[LOW-CONF]**; \"specific tempi inferred\" is the research's own wording. Also inferred: the key list, every velocity and register bound, the `intervals`/`contour` split and the hi-hat weights. ⚠ `snareRoll.pitchWalk` is inherited.",
